# The home page: the first screen, the note, the story, the numbers, how to join and what it costs.

home-meta-title = 333
home-meta-description = 333 is a three-byte file kept alive by people who leave a small program running. It lives as long as somebody is awake to pass it on.
home-meta-og-description = 333. It lives as long as somebody is awake to pass it on.

home-hero-line = It lives as long as somebody is awake to pass it on.
home-take = Take the program
home-see-network = The network
home-hero-foot = <span data-figure="answering">{ $answering }</span> answering in epoch <span data-figure="epoch">{ $epoch }</span>
home-note-label = A note first
home-note-title = A note first, in plain words. These are the only plain words on this site.
home-note = To someone who truly believed it, this could become a religion. I am not a mad cult leader, so please do not take me for one. I am someone who loves technology. This is a toy project I built once, and the whole thing is a bit. It is a concept, played straight. Everywhere else on this site, in the repository, and in the program itself, I stay in character.
home-story-label = How 333 is kept
home-story-1 = The file is three bytes, and they read 333.
home-story-1-drawn = It is named 333.txt, and each of the three bytes is 0x33.
home-story-2 = Nobody gets in alone. Somebody who has the file hands it to you, and the two of you sign for it.
home-story-2-drawn = The one who hands it over signs “{ js-story-gave }”, and the one who receives it signs “{ js-story-received }”.
home-story-3 = Every epoch of 333 minutes, three other nodes are drawn to ask whether yours is awake.
home-story-4 = What they sign is the record that you were.
home-story-4-drawn = The record keeps one mark for each of the last { js-story-epochs }, up to { js-story-now }.
home-story-5 = The number that matters is how many are answering, not how many names are on the roll.
home-story-6 = When it reaches zero, a count of 19,683 years begins. One answer ends it.
home-now-title = The network, as this site's node sees it
home-now-note = Every node sees only what it has verified itself. Somebody else's node sees something else.
home-see-map = Where we are
home-join-title = Take the program
home-join-lede = 333 is a small program you leave running on a computer you can spare. On Linux or a Mac, in a terminal:
home-join-others = Windows, Light, and installing by hand are on <a href="{ $base }/start">Take the program</a>.
home-ask-title = Then ask this site's node for the file
home-ask = The node that runs this site is awake at all hours. It hands the file to whoever asks, and the two nodes sign for it.
home-ask-others = If somebody you know already has the file, ask them instead. Their invitation works the same way, and this site is not needed at all.
home-cost-title = What it costs
home-cost-1 = A machine left on, because an hour it is off is an hour nobody can count.
home-cost-2 = The address it speaks from. Every node it answers sees it. Whoever runs the meeting point at this site learns it, works out the country, and places it on <a href="{ $base }/map">the public map</a> rounded to whole degrees, unless your node answers only through Tor.
home-cost-3 = And one directory, holding the name, that cannot be replaced if it is lost. There is no account to recover and nobody to ask.
